\description{
\if{html}{\out{<a href="https://adrientaudiere.github.io/MiscMetabar/articles/Rules.html#lifecycle">}} \if{html}{\out{<img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/lifecycle-experimental-orange" alt="lifecycle-experimental">}}\if{html}{\out{</a>}}

Run a \emph{phylogenetic placement} of the \code{refseq} slot of a
\code{\link[phyloseq]{phyloseq-class}} object onto a fixed reference
phylogeny with EPA-ng (Barbera et al. 2019). Rather than building a tree
from the metabarcoding reads themselves — which short, fast-evolving markers
rarely support — each query sequence is grafted onto the branch of a curated
reference tree it fits best, with a likelihood weight attached.

The three steps are chained for you:
\enumerate{
\item the query sequences are aligned \strong{into} the reference alignment with
\code{mafft --add --keeplength}, so that they end up on exactly the reference's
columns, which EPA-ng requires;
\item EPA-ng places them on the reference tree and writes a \code{.jplace} file;
\item the \code{.jplace} file is read back with \code{\link[BoSSA:read_jplace]{BoSSA::read_jplace()}} and flattened
into a data.frame.
}

Use \code{\link[=ref_package_pq]{ref_package_pq()}} to assemble the reference and \code{\link[=assign_placement_pq]{assign_placement_pq()}}
to turn the placements into a taxonomy.
}
\details{
EPA-ng is an external program and is not installed with phylopq.
The quickest route is conda (\verb{conda install -c bioconda epa-ng});
\code{\link[=install_epang]{install_epang()}} builds it from source instead.

The likelihood weight ratio (LWR) of a placement is the share of the total
likelihood that branch accounts for; it is the number to filter on. A
query spread thinly over many branches is placed with little confidence,
whatever its best branch is, which is why \code{best_only = FALSE} is worth
inspecting before trusting an assignment.
}
\section{The reference is the science}{

Placement never questions the reference tree — it only asks where a query
fits on it. Everything therefore rests on that reference, and the two halves
of it answer to different constraints:
\itemize{
\item \strong{The reference alignment must match your primers.} It has to be the same
locus, over a comparable region, as the amplicon you sequenced. This is
the constraint that cannot be relaxed: placing ITS2 reads on an ITS1
reference yields confident-looking nonsense.
\item \strong{The reference tree need not come from that locus at all.} Being fixed,
it is the natural place to inject evidence a single barcode cannot carry —
a multi-locus phylogeny, a topology constrained by morphology, ecology or
mating data, the published tree of an integrative-taxonomy study. Short
markers rarely resolve deep relationships; a tree built from several loci
and morpho-ecological characters does, and the placement inherits that
resolution.
}

In practice the reference package is almost always \strong{external} to the
phyloseq object: curated vouchers, not your own ASVs. Three arrangements are
supported, all through \code{\link[=ref_package_pq]{ref_package_pq()}}:
\enumerate{
\item a fully external reference — sequences \emph{and} tree from a database or a
published study, every taxon of \code{physeq} a query;
\item external reference sequences with the tree inferred from them, when no
published phylogeny exists for the group;
\item a mixed reference — a few securely identified taxa of your own dataset
added to an external reference to thicken the clade your queries fall
into, the rest placed with \code{exclude_taxa}.
}
}

